  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA warm front will lift north Sunday night ahead of the upper level \nridge which will expand into the region. High temperatures will \ntrend 3-5 degrees warmer on Monday then continue to creep up Tuesday \nand Wednesday as the upper level ridge becomes firmly established. \nTemperatures at 850mb will range from 20-23C with highs in northern \nOhio in the lower 90s, with a few sites in NW Ohio reaching the mid \n90s. This is 10 or more degrees above normal for the beginning of \nSeptember. Dewpoints also increase during this time frame into the \nupper 60s which will push heat index values solidly into the mid and \nupper 90s Tuesday and potentially approaching 100 across the western \nhalf of the area on Wednesday. (Heat index will be slightly\ncooler in the lower 90s in NW PA). It is possible that a \nportion of the area could need a Heat Advisory by Tuesday or \nWednesday but will also depend on if we have any thunderstorms \nthose days. In general confidence is increase that there will be\na heat threat early this week but some adjustment to temperatures\nis still possible based on clouds and coverage of any \nprecipitation. \n \nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure with a dry airmass is in place across the region \ntoday before building to the Mid-Atlantic region overnight. \nMeanwhile mid-level moisture will be on the increase overnight \nas shortwave energy traverses the Great Lakes Region. While low \nlevels are generally expected to remain dry, models do not have \na very good handle on the southern extent of showers in Illinois\nthis afternoon. While the precipitation across the western \nGreat Lakes is largely expected to dissipate, expect sufficient \nmoistening aloft to include a 20 percent chance of a shower \nacross Lake Erie or a few of our northern counties overnight, \nwhich could just be in the form of sprinkles. \n\nBetter chances for precipitation come Sunday night as convection \nover lower Michigan favors a southeast trajectory towards Northwest \nPennsylvania ahead of the building ridge. The warm front will bisect \nour northeastern counties Sunday night before lifting north on \nMonday. There is some potential for locally heavy rain across mainly \nNorthwest Pennsylvania on Sunday night with thunderstorms. After \nthat the ridge is expected to expand north on Monday. This will \nhopefully place the active storm track from the Upper Great Lakes \ntowards New York but we will have to monitor the strength of the \nbuilding ridge on Tuesday and Wednesday. Models generally in good \nagreement of 700mb temperatures reaching 10C with a capping \ninversion developing overhead. The next stronger piece of energy to \nround or break down the upper level ridge is expected Wednesday \nnight, pushing a weak cold front south into the area by \nThursday. We will have to monitor the eventual break down of the\nupper level ridge which could lead to strong thunderstorms for \na portion of the area on Wednesday night or Thursday.",
